  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1202 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A beautiful Fall day continues across the region as \nwe're seeing plenty of sunshine, temperatures above normal, and \ngenerally light winds.  In fact, the latest NBM continues to be a \nfew degrees too cool for highs, so have adjusted upwards. This trend \nlikely continues Monday as well. \n\nTONIGHT: Weak southwesterly surface winds will develop later this \nevening and continue overnight.  While broad warm advection \ncontinues above the ground, there may be some potential for \nvalley/river fog into Monday morning.  The general increase in mid-\nupr clouds could temper this potential somewhat. \n\nMONDAY-TUESDAY: Extremely quiet weather continues early this week. A \nfrontal boundary will swing southeast across the Tri-State area \nearly Monday morning, turning a light southwesterly wind \ntowards the west-northwest in the afternoon. Not a ton of cold \nair behind this boundary, so anticipating highs rising into the \n60s in most locations. High pressure passing through the Plains \nMonday night shifts southeast of the area by Tuesday. \nAdditionally sfc cyclogenesis over the Western High Plains will \naid in inducing a southwesterly boundary layer flow on Tuesday. \nTemperatures will again rise into the upper 50s in most areas. \nOne area to watch in this timeframe would be a repeated risk for\nvalley fog Tuesday morning as winds may be very light and the \nlow-lvl ridge remains west of the CWA. HRRR/RRFS both indicate \nincreased potential for lowered visibilities. \n\nTUESDAY NIGHT-WEDNESDAY:  The only PoPs in the 7 day forecast are \nfocused in the late Tuesday night and Wednesday timeframe. A \nprogressive open shortwave leaves the Pacific Northwest and reaches \nthe Plains Tuesday night.  This moisture starved wave could lead to \na few sprinkles into Wednesday morning.  A deeper upper trough \ncentered over Hudson Bay will allow a second shortwave to pivot \nsouthward out of central Canada and into the Plains on Wednesday. \nAgain not a ton of moisture with this system through the column, but \nperhaps a brief window early Wednesday where sprinkles to a very \nlight rain shower will be possible.  Ensemble probabilities remain \nlow, with measurable rain chances between 20-40%, and probabilities \nof >0.10\" near zero. NBM probabilities are likely too low, \nwhich is understandable given spread in models. Either way, not \na highly impactful system. \n\nTHURSDAY-SUNDAY: Arctic high pressure arrives into the Plains for \nThursday. This 1033 mb high pushes a shallow but meaningful \ndrop in temperatures southwards, with highs on Thursday favored\nto be in the lower 40s Thursday and Friday. Some of the coldest\nair of the fall season may arrive Friday morning with lows \npotentially in the lower to middle 20s. 25/75th percentile data\nfrom the LREF suggests a wider range of possibilities (>10 \ndegree spread) west of the James River which is the result of \nuncertain low-lvl warm advection return. \n\nMid-lvl heights grow through next weekend, with strong indications \nof a continued dry forecast through Sunday.  Temperatures will also \nbegin to rise after the arctic high passes likely at or above \nnormal.  A 15 degree spread in the 25/75th high temperature forecast \nthrough the weekend may be the result of some uncertainty on the \neastward progression of the mid-lvl ridge and continued influence of \ncooler SE surface flow.  Regardless, near to above normal \ntemperatures are favored.",
